1. (i) It is translation and scale invariant, in contrast to 𝗌𝖬𝖠𝖯𝖤\mathsf{sMAPE} and the version of the valid time used in [[Ren+09](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#bib.bibx22), [Pat+18](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#bib.bibx20)], but similar to our definition of t𝗏𝖺𝗅𝗂𝖽t\_{\mathsf{valid}}. This is desirable as every ODE system can be modified — without adding complexity — to create any translation and scaling of its solutions. Thus, the evaluation of an estimator u^\hat{u} should not depend on the location and scale of the system. 2. (ii) We focus on a task that involves evaluating the precise state within a near-future time interval, as in weather forecasting. In contrast, one could also consider a task where the long-term behavior of the prediction should resemble the ground truth, without emphasizing the error at any single point in time, as in climate modeling. For the former task, predictive power deteriorates quickly over time due to the chaotic nature of the systems considered. Therefore, if the prediction interval [T,T+S][T,T+S] is long, we are primarily interested in the early times when accurate prediction is possible. Accurate predictions at the beginning of the interval should not be discounted by divergence at the end, where accurate predictions are not possible anyway. This requirement is captured by 𝖢𝖬𝖤\mathsf{CME} and, to some extent, by t𝗏𝖺𝗅𝗂𝖽t\_{\mathsf{valid}}, but not by 𝗌𝖬𝖠𝖯𝖤\mathsf{sMAPE}. 3. (iii) The value of the 𝖢𝖬𝖤\mathsf{CME} is always between 0 and 1, where 0 is only achieved by perfect prediction. In contrast, the scale of t𝗏𝖺𝗅𝗂𝖽t\_{\mathsf{valid}} depends strongly on the system’s time scale and the best value t𝗏𝖺𝗅𝗂𝖽=St\_{\mathsf{valid}}=S can be achieved for imperfect predictions. 4. (iv) Dealing with missing values when calculating the 𝖢𝖬𝖤\mathsf{CME} is canonical: If u^​(t)\hat{u}(t) is not available for some tt, we set the value of the minimum in ([5](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#S2.E5 "Equ