### 3.9 Using The Cumulative Maximum Error Is Advantageous

When ranking different methods based on their performance, the error metrics 𝖢𝖬𝖤\mathsf{CME}, 𝗌𝖬𝖠𝖯𝖤\mathsf{sMAPE}, and t𝗏𝖺𝗅𝗂𝖽t\_{\mathsf{valid}} generally produce similar results. However, a notable difference arises with 𝗌𝖬𝖠𝖯𝖤\mathsf{sMAPE} for more challenging tasks, where strong initial performance can be overshadowed by a divergence of the forecast later on (instead of, e.g., defaulting to a constant), see [Table 13](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T13 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems"). As seen from the explicit error values in [Table 14](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T14 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems"), t𝗏𝖺𝗅𝗂𝖽t\_{\mathsf{valid}} is less effective at distinguishing well-performing methods when the testing duration is too short or the threshold parameter is too lenient, as several methods can achieve the maximum valid time. The 𝖢𝖬𝖤\mathsf{CME} does not suffer from any of these limitations.

