* • Lorenz63std: The Lorenz63 system in its standard definition [[Lor63](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#bib.bibx17)], i.e., | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | f⁡(u)=(σ⁡(u2−u1)u1​(ρ−u3)−u2u1​u2−β​u3)f(u)=\begin{pmatrix}\sigma(u\_{2}-u\_{1})\\ u\_{1}(\rho-u\_{3})-u\_{2}\\ u\_{1}u\_{2}-\beta u\_{3}\end{pmatrix} | | (12) | with σ=10\sigma=10, ρ=28\rho=28, β=8/3\beta=8/3. * • Lorenz63random: As Lorenz63std, but for each replication, the three parameters are drawn uniformly at random from an interval, | | | | | | --- | --- | --- | --- | | | σ∼𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿⁡([5,15]),ρ∼𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿⁡([20,80]),β∼𝖴𝗇𝗂𝖿⁡([2,6]).\sigma\sim\mathsf{Unif}([5,15])\,,\quad\rho\sim\mathsf{Unif}([20,80])\,,\quad\beta\sim\mathsf{Unif}([2,6])\,. | | (13) | These intervals are chosen so that the resulting systems exhibit chaotic behavior. * • Lorenz63nonpar: As Lorenz63std, but the parameters depend on the state, σ=σ⁡(u)\sigma=\sigma(u), ρ=ρ⁡(u)\rho=\rho(u), β=β⁡(u)\beta=\beta(u). For each replication, the functions σ,ρ,β:ℝ3→ℝ\sigma,\rho,\beta\colon\mathbb{R}^{3}\to\mathbb{R} are sampled from a Gaussian process so that the (non-constant) parameter values are in (or at least close to) the intervals sampled from in ([13](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A1.E13 "Equation 13 ‣ 2nd item ‣ A.2 DeebLorenz ‣ Appendix A The Databases ‣ Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems")). To make sure that the sampled vector fields lead to interesting systems, we reject results where the trajectory of given initial states seem to approach a fixed point. The resulting trajectories all seem to exhibit chaotic behavior.

