## Appendix D Further Details of the Results

In this section we list additional tables and plots the show the results of the simulation study in more detail. Mean error values (𝖢𝖬𝖤\mathsf{CME}, 𝗌𝖬𝖠𝖯𝖤\mathsf{sMAPE}, t𝗏𝖺𝗅𝗂𝖽t\_{\mathsf{valid}}) for DeebLorenz are shown in [Tables 7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T7 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems"), [8](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T8 "Table 8 ‣ Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems") and [9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T9 "Table 9 ‣ Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems"). The respective rankings are displayed in [Tables 10](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T10 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems"), [11](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T11 "Table 11 ‣ Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems") and [12](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T12 "Table 12 ‣ Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems"). Furthermore, [Table 13](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T13 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems") shows the ranks for all error metrics side by side and highlights the strongest disagreement. For Dysts, the median error values over all systems are shown in [Table 14](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T14 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems"). These tables also include the values of the error metrics on the tuning data. [Table 15](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T15 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems") and [Table 16](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T16 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems") detail the performances of the different variants of propagator based methods. Results of statistical tests for distinguishing the performances of methods on DeebLorenz are shown in [Figure 7](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.F7 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems") and [Figure 8](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.F8 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems"). [Figures 9](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.F9 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems"), [10](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.F10 "Figure 10 ‣ Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems"), [11](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.F11 "Figure 11 ‣ Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems"), [12](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.F12 "Figure 12 ‣ Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems") and [13](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.F13 "Figure 13 ‣ Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems") show variants of [Figure 4](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#S3.F4 "In 3 Results and 10 Key Insights ‣ Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems") for the other systems and observation schemes of DeebLorenz. Finally, [Table 19](https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2407.20158#A4.T19 "In Machine Learning for Predicting Chaotic Systems") displays results for the system Lorenz63std of DeebLorenz with reduced and increased data size.

